<p class="sst-lead">An aerodynamic model depends on many shape parameters \(x\in\mathbb{R}^m\), yet the quantity of
interest often varies along only a <strong>handful of directions</strong>.</p>

<p>\[ C = \int_{\mathcal{X}} \nabla f(x)\,\nabla f(x)^{\top} \rho(x)\,dx = W\Lambda W^{\top}. \]</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>The dominant eigenvectors span an <strong>active subspace</strong>: \(f(x)\approx g(W_r^{\top}x)\), a
  <em>ridge function</em> &mdash; flat across the inactive directions.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">This is the linear precursor. It has a catch: the active subspace depends on how you
  <em>parametrized</em> the shape &mdash; which is what pushes us toward an intrinsic, geometric description.</p>
</div>
